# Use Bun, not Node.js

Default to using Bun instead of Node.js, npm, pnpm, yarn, or vite.

## CLI / commands

- Use `bun <file>` instead of `node <file>` or `ts-node <file>`.
- Use `bun install` instead of `npm install`, `yarn install`, or `pnpm install`.
- Use `bun add <pkg>` / `bun remove <pkg>` instead of the npm/yarn/pnpm equivalents.
- Use `bun run <script>` instead of `npm run`, `yarn run`, or `pnpm run`.
- Use `bunx <pkg>` instead of `npx <pkg>`.
- Use `bun test` instead of `jest` or `vitest`.
- Use `bun build <file>` instead of `webpack`, `esbuild`, or `rollup`.
- Use `bun audit` instead of `npm audit`, `yarn audit`, or `pnpm audit`.
- Use `bun --hot <file>` for a hot-reloading dev process; `bun --watch <file>` to restart on change.

## Runtime APIs — prefer Bun's built-ins

- Bun automatically loads `.env`, so DON'T use `dotenv` or `import "dotenv/config"`.
- `Bun.serve()` supports HTTP, routes, WebSockets, and HTTPS. Prefer it over `express`. Don't use `ws` — WebSocket support is built in.
- `bun:sqlite` for SQLite — prefer it over `better-sqlite3`.
- `Bun.redis` is a built-in Redis client — prefer it over `ioredis`.
- `Bun.sql` is a built-in Postgres client — prefer it over `pg` or `postgres.js`.
- `Bun.s3` / `Bun.S3Client` is a built-in S3 client that works with AWS S3 and any S3-compatible storage (Cloudflare R2, MinIO, etc.). Prefer it over `@aws-sdk/client-s3`. Credentials are read from `.env` (`S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY`, `S3_BUCKET`, `S3_ENDPOINT`, `S3_REGION`) automatically.
- `Bun.file(path)` for reading/writing files — prefer it over `node:fs`'s `readFile`/`writeFile`.
- Use `Bun.$\`command\`` (Bun Shell) for running shell commands — prefer it over `execa` or `child_process`.
- `WebSocket` and `fetch` are built into the global scope — no need to install `ws` or `node-fetch`.

## Testing

Use `bun test` to run tests. Write tests with Bun's built-in test runner:

```ts
import { test, expect } from "bun:test";

test("example", () => {
  expect(1 + 1).toBe(2);
});
```

## Frontend

Bun can directly run and bundle frontend code — you usually don't need vite.

- Bun supports importing `.html`, `.css`, `.svg`, and other assets directly.
- `Bun.serve()` can serve a fullstack app: pass HTML imports to the `routes` option and Bun bundles `<script>` / `<link>` tags automatically.

```ts
import index from "./index.html";

Bun.serve({
  routes: {
    "/": index,
    "/api/hello": () => new Response("Hello via Bun!"),
  },
});
```

Use `bun run <file.html>` to spin up a dev server with hot reloading for a frontend entry point.

## S3

```ts
// Read / write objects
const file = Bun.s3.file("path/to/object.json");
const data = await file.json();
await Bun.s3.write("uploads/report.pdf", pdfBytes);

// Presigned URL (e.g. for a download link valid 1 hour)
const url = Bun.s3.presign("uploads/report.pdf", { expiresIn: 3600 });

// Custom endpoint (R2 / MinIO / other S3-compatible)
import { S3Client } from "bun";
const client = new S3Client({
  endpoint: "https://<account>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com",
  bucket: "my-bucket",
});
```

## Notes

- Bun reads `bun.lock` (or `bun.lockb`). It ignores `package-lock.json`, `yarn.lock`, and `pnpm-lock.yaml`.
- For TypeScript, no separate build step is needed to run files — `bun run file.ts` works directly.